  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 1874171244544
    m_Localized: Glucagon is one of the hormones produced by the pancreas, which
      acts on liver cells that store glycogen (a source of glucose reserve in animals).
      When blood glucose levels drop, the hormone glucagon, stored in the pancreas
      as proglucagon, is released into the bloodstream and enters liver cells, initiating
      the process of breaking down glycogen, releasing glucose molecules.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1
    m_Localized: The immune system is composed of various cells, including phagocytes
      and lymphocytes. The former are represented by Neutrophils (also known as polymorphonuclear
      cells) and Monocytes, present in the blood, and by macrophages present in various
      tissues of our body. Lymphocytes are found in the blood and also in lymph nodes
      scattered throughout the body, and they are involved in antibody production.
      Foreign particles (bacteria, protozoa, viruses) can be engulfed by phagocytes
      and degraded by enzymes present in lysosomes. Acid hydrolase is one of the
      enzymes contained in lysosomes that is involved in the degradation of phagocytosed
      microorganisms.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2
    m_Localized: In the digestive process, most of the carbohydrates present in our
      diet are converted into glucose, a simple sugar that is the primary source
      of energy used in all cellular processes. However, the glucose absorbed during
      digestion and found freely circulating in the bloodstream cannot penetrate
      into cells. For this to occur, the cell requires the hormone insulin, which
      is produced by the pancreas, but in the form of a precursor to insulin called
      proinsulin, which is stored in specific cells. Moments after meals, proinsulin
      molecules are converted into insulin, released into the bloodstream, and distributed
      to cells throughout the body.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3
    m_Localized: Melanin is produced by melanocytes, one of the types of cells found
      in the skin, whose main function is pigmentation and protection against solar
      radiation. It provides pigmentation to the skin, eyes, and hair across all
      biological kingdoms. The absence of melanin gives rise to a condition called
      albinism in the animal kingdom.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
